{"subjectId":"sql","id":"employee-manager-queries","title":"Employee–Manager Queries","group":"Interview Prep","order":58,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The self-referencing <code>employees</code> table powers a whole family of interview questions. All of them are a self join, an aggregate, or a recursive CTE.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-up-right\"></i>Employees earning more than their manager</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name AS employee, e.salary, m.name AS manager, m.salary AS manager_salary\nFROM employees e\nJOIN employees m ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nWHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | salary | manager | manager_salary\n---------+--------+---------+---------------\nSara     | 85000  | Vikram  | 80000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-users-group\"></i>Managers with at least N direct reports</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT m.name AS manager, COUNT(*) AS direct_reports\nFROM employees e\nJOIN employees m ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nGROUP BY m.emp_id, m.name\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 2\nORDER BY direct_reports DESC, manager;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>manager | direct_reports\n--------+---------------\nAsha    | 4\nPriya   | 2\nRavi    | 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-leaf\"></i>Employees who are not managers (leaf nodes)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name\nFROM employees e\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees r WHERE r.manager_id = e.emp_id)\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-----\nMeera\nKaran\nArjun\nNeha\nSara\nDev</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-id-badge-2\"></i>Employee, manager and department in one row</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name AS employee,\n       COALESCE(m.name, '—')      AS manager,\n       COALESCE(d.name, 'Unassigned') AS department,\n       CASE WHEN e.dept_id = m.dept_id THEN 'same' ELSE 'different' END AS manager_dept\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN employees m   ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nLEFT JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | manager | department  | manager_dept\n---------+---------+-------------+-------------\nAsha     | —       | Engineering | different\nRavi     | Asha    | Engineering | same\nMeera    | Ravi    | Engineering | same\nKaran    | Ravi    | Engineering | same\nPriya    | Asha    | Sales       | different\nArjun    | Priya   | Sales       | same\nNeha     | Priya   | Sales       | same\nVikram   | Asha    | HR          | different\nSara     | Vikram  | HR          | same\nDev      | Asha    | Unassigned  | different</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-percentage\"></i>Salary as a percentage of the manager's</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name, e.salary, m.name AS manager,\n       ROUND(100.0 * e.salary / m.salary, 1) AS pct_of_manager\nFROM employees e JOIN employees m ON m.emp_id = e.manager_id\nORDER BY pct_of_manager DESC, e.emp_id\nLIMIT 4;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | manager | pct_of_manager\n------+--------+---------+---------------\nSara  | 85000  | Vikram  | 106.3\nRavi  | 120000 | Asha    | 80.0\nMeera | 95000  | Ravi    | 79.2\nKaran | 95000  | Ravi    | 79.2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Department salary above company average</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT d.name, ROUND(AVG(e.salary)) AS dept_avg\nFROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.name\nHAVING AVG(e.salary) &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)\nORDER BY dept_avg DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name        | dept_avg\n------------+---------\nEngineering | 115000.0</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find the full reporting chain of an employee?</summary><div><p>A recursive CTE: anchor on the employee, then repeatedly join <code>employees m ON m.emp_id = chain.manager_id</code> until <code>manager_id</code> is <code>NULL</code> — see <em>Recursive CTEs</em>. For a fixed depth, chain self joins (<code>e → m1 → m2</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}